    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d331">

                <pb facs="0358" n="346" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg346"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760D.61/1516: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-09-25T19:00:00+03:00">September 25,
                            1940—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 26—12:57 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1232. The Moscow press today contains no reference to Finland. The sudden
                    recrudescence yesterday of anti-Finnish items in the press<anchor n="62"
                        xml:id="d331-f1"/> after a month’s silence on the subject may have been due
                    to <pb facs="0359" n="347" xml:id="pg347"/> Soviet knowledge of the Finnish
                    agreement with Germany<anchor n="63" xml:id="d331-f2"/> announced on the British
                    radio today to permit German troops to pass through Finland en route to Norway.
                    Although the paper in question has not yet been received by the Embassy, I am
                    informed by an American correspondent that a recent edition of a paper in
                    Petrozavodsk, Soviet Karelia, contained a violent attack upon Finland, asserting
                    that the Society for Friendship and Peace with the Soviet Union is only
                    prevented from becoming an overwhelming mass movement in Finland by the
                    terroristic and repressive actions of the Finnish Government.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Helsinki.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d331-f1" n="62" xml:id="d331-f1-content">
                <p>The Ambassador in the Soviet Union had noted in his telegram No. 1217, September
                    24, 2 p.m., that the Moscow press had published seven news items critical of
                    conditions in Finland. There was also a short announcement of the opening of
                    direct rail and passenger traffic on September 23, between Finland and the
                    Soviet Union, in accordance with the agreement of September 6, 1940.
                    (760D.61/1515)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d331-f2" n="63" xml:id="d331-f2-content">
                <p>The German-Finnish agreement for the transit of German troops through Finland to
                    Norway was signed on September 22, 1940; it was announced in the Soviet press in
                    a Tass despatch from Berlin on September 28. For text, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Documents on German Foreign Policy, 1918–1945</hi>,
                    series D, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">xi</hi>, Doc. No. 86.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
